<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🏙️ City Guides</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        How Many Days Do You Need in China? Itinerary by Trip Length
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Plan the perfect China trip. From a 3-day whirlwind to a 3-week adventure — find the itinerary that fits your timeline.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: June 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-50 to-indigo-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-900 mb-4">⚡ Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-blue-800 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>China deserves at least 5–7 days.</strong> If you only have 3 days, stick to one city (Beijing or Shanghai). With 7–10 days, you can experience both the classic sights and a second contrasting city. Two weeks unlocks the full diversity — history, nature, food, and modern China all in one trip. Here's how to match your itinerary to your available time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#3-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🕐 3 Days: One City, Maximum Impact</a></li>
        <li><a href="#5-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🕐 5 Days: Slow Down or Add a Second City</a></li>
        <li><a href="#7-10-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🕐 7–10 Days: The Classic China Route</a></li>
        <li><a href="#10-14-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🕐 10–14 Days: Deep Dive</a></li>
        <li><a href="#14-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🕐 14+ Days: The Grand Tour</a></li>
        <li><a href="#which-city" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏙️ Which City Fits You?</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="3-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🕐 3 Days: One City, Maximum Impact</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Three days is tight, but it's enough to fall in love with China. Pick one city and commit — you will see more of it than most travelers who race through five cities in a week. You will also save yourself the headache of train stations and check-ins every other day.</p>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Option A: Beijing</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Pick Beijing if you want to touch 600 years of Chinese history with your own hands. The Forbidden City is the world's largest palace complex — 980 buildings, 9,000 rooms, and the kind of scale that makes you feel small. The Great Wall snakes across mountains an hour and a half outside town. And the hutongs (ancient alleyways) are still lived in by real Beijing families. You are not visiting a museum. You are visiting a city that has been China's center for half a millennium.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Day 1: Forbidden City (3 hours minimum) + Tiananmen Square + Jingshan Park for sunset over the palace roofs</li>
        <li>Day 2: Mutianyu Great Wall (full-day trip — it's 1.5 hours each way from the city, but the toboggan ride down is worth it)</li>
        <li>Day 3: Temple of Heaven at dawn (watch locals practicing tai chi) + hutong walk + Lama Temple</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500">Best for: History lovers, first-timers to Asia</p>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Option B: Shanghai</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Pick Shanghai if you want to see the China of tomorrow. The skyline along the Bund is unlike anything in the West — a megalopolis that went from fishing village to global financial hub in 150 years. The food scene is incredible, from soup dumplings at 50-year-old hole-in-the-wall shops to world-class cocktail bars in the Wukang Road area. English is more widely spoken here than anywhere else in China. If this is your first trip to Asia and you are nervous, Shanghai is the gentlest landing.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Day 1: Yu Garden with its classical Ming dynasty pavilions (morning) → The Bund waterfront walk (afternoon) → Huangpu River cruise at sunset + Nanjing Road night lights</li>
        <li>Day 2: Shanghai Tower observation deck for a clear skyline view (morning) → Zhujiajiao Water Town afternoon trip (1h by metro from city center) → return to central Shanghai for evening</li>
        <li>Day 3: Wukang Road and Anfu Road area for cafés and boutique shopping (morning) → free time near People's Square → head to airport</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500">Best for: Modern architecture, foodies, nightlife, first-timers nervous about culture shock</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="5-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🕐 5 Days: Slow Down or Add a Second City</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">With five days, you can either go deeper into one city or squeeze in a second destination. The right choice depends entirely on how you travel. If you hate packing and unpacking, stay put. If you get itchy staying in one place, the Beijing + Xi'an duo is unbeatable.</p>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Option A: Beijing Slow (5 days)</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Three days in Beijing leaves you wanting more. Five days lets you slow down. You can spend a morning wandering the Summer Palace's lakefront instead of rushing through it. You can get lost in 798 Art District — a sprawling contemporary art scene housed in a former factory complex. You can even take a day trip to Chengde, where Qing emperors escaped the summer heat in a sprawling mountain resort that blends Chinese, Tibetan, and Mongolian architecture.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Day 4: Summer Palace (half day) + 798 Art District afternoon</li>
        <li>Day 5: Pick one — cooking class, tea ceremony, or Chengde day trip (2h train from Beijing)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Option B: Classic Duo — Beijing + Xi'an</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">This is the best two-city hit in China. Beijing gives you the imperial capital — the Forbidden City, the Great Wall. Xi'an gives you China's ancient soul — the Terracotta Warriors, the best-preserved city wall in the world, and a Muslim Quarter that smells of lamb skewers and cumin. The high-speed train takes 4.5 hours. You can leave Beijing after breakfast and be eating lunch in Xi'an. This route works because these two cities are different enough that the contrast itself becomes the experience.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Days 1–3: Beijing (Forbidden City, Great Wall, Hutongs)</li>
        <li>Day 4: Morning train to Xi'an (4.5h). Afternoon at the Terracotta Warriors — 8,000 life-sized soldiers, each with a unique face, standing in battle formation for 2,200 years. Evening food crawl through the Muslim Quarter.</li>
        <li>Day 5: Rent a bike and ride the full 14km loop of the Ancient City Wall. Visit the Big Wild Goose Pagoda before heading home.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500">Best for: History buffs who want two iconic stops</p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="7-10-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🕐 7–10 Days: The Classic China Route</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Seven to ten days gives you enough time to see three cities and start making sense of China's scale. You will see the imperial capital (Beijing), the ancient capital (Xi'an), and the commercial capital (Shanghai). They are connected by high-speed rail, so you spend your time exploring, not sitting in airport lounges.</p>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Recommended Route: Beijing → Xi'an → Shanghai</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">This is the Golden Triangle for a reason. It covers 3,000 years of Chinese history, from the Terracotta Warriors (221 BC) to the Forbidden City (1420) to the Shanghai Tower (2015). Each city feels completely different. Beijing is political and monumental. Xi'an is ancient and earthy. Shanghai is modern and sleek. The three together tell the full story of China in a way a single city cannot.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Beijing (3 days): Forbidden City, Great Wall, Hutongs — the imperial heart of China</li>
        <li>Xi'an (2 days): Terracotta Warriors, City Wall bike ride, Muslim Quarter — China's ancient soul</li>
        <li>Shanghai (3 days): The Bund, Xuhui District (Wukang Road area), Zhujiajiao Water Town — China's future</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Variation with Nature: Beijing → Chengdu → Shanghai</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Swap Xi'an for Chengdu if pandas matter more to you than the Terracotta Warriors. Chengdu is China's laid-back southwest — slower pace, spicier food, and a tea house culture that makes you want to sit still for an afternoon. The Giant Panda Breeding Base is the best place in the world to see pandas up close. And Sichuan food is not just spicy — it is a whole cuisine built around the numbing sensation of Sichuan peppercorns. This route works well for travelers who want less ancient history and more food, nature, and everyday Chinese life.</p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
        <li>Beijing (3 days): Forbidden City, Great Wall, Temple of Heaven</li>
        <li>Chengdu (3 days): Pandas at dawn (they are most active in the morning), Jinli Ancient Street, Sichuan hot pot at night, a lazy afternoon in a tea house in People's Park</li>
        <li>Shanghai (2 days): The Bund, Wukang Road area cafés and shops, a final taste of megacity energy before flying home</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 id="10-14-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🕐 10–14 Days: Deep Dive</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">With two weeks, you can see the big four — Beijing, Xi'an, Chengdu, and Shanghai — and add a natural wonder on top. This is the trip most first-time visitors end up wishing they had planned. You get the history (Beijing + Xi'an), the food and culture (Chengdu), the landscape (Guilin), and the modern metropolis (Shanghai).</p>

    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Suggested 14-Day Route: Beijing → Xi'an → Chengdu → Guilin → Shanghai</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">The big addition here is Guilin, where the landscape looks like a Chinese brush painting come to life — limestone karst peaks rising out of misty rivers, rice paddies stretching to the horizon, and water buffalo wading through flooded fields. The Li River cruise is the closest thing to floating through a painting. This route does not waste a single day; every city offers something completely different from the one before it. Fly between Chengdu and Guilin (direct flights available), then Guilin to Shanghai.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-gray-700">
          <thead><tr class="border-b"><th class="py-2 pr-4">Day</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">City</th><th class="py-2">Highlights</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">1–3</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Beijing</td><td class="py-2">Walk through 600 years of imperial history. Forbidden City, Great Wall, Hutongs, Summer Palace</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">4–5</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Xi'an</td><td class="py-2">Stand face-to-face with the Terracotta Warriors. Bike the 14km City Wall. Eat your way through the Muslim Quarter</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">6–8</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Chengdu</td><td class="py-2">Watch pandas before they nap (go at 8 AM). Eat the best Sichuan food of your life. Spend an afternoon doing nothing in a tea house</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">9–11</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Guilin / Yangshuo</td><td class="py-2">Sail the Li River through karst peaks that look impossible. Cycle through rice paddies. Go bamboo rafting on the Yulong River</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">12–14</td><td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Shanghai</td><td class="py-2">End in China's most foreigner-friendly city. The Bund, Wukang Road and the Xuhui District, Zhujiajiao Water Town, one last incredible meal</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="14-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🕐 14+ Days: The Grand Tour</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">If you have three weeks or more, you can add depth. Instead of rushing through cities, you can spend 4-5 days in each place, take day trips, and actually live in a city instead of touring it. Here is where experienced return travelers go:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
      <li><strong>Guilin/Yangshuo</strong> (3–4 days): The landscape that inspired a thousand Chinese paintings. Cruise the Li River, then cycle through rice paddies in Yangshuo. The karst mountains are surreal — limestone peaks wrapped in mist. Go bamboo rafting on the Yulong River for the quietest, most beautiful hour of your trip.</li>
      <li><strong>Guangzhou + Shenzhen</strong> (3–4 days): Cantonese food capital of the world. Dim sum in Guangzhou is a religious experience — carts of shrimp dumplings, chicken feet, and custard buns rolling past your table from 7 AM. Shenzhen is a day trip for the tech museums and modern architecture. Hong Kong is 20 minutes by high-speed rail from Shenzhen if you want to add it.</li>
      <li><strong>Chengdu + Sichuan</strong> (5–7 days): Chengdu is a gateway to some of China's best natural scenery. Jiuzhaigou National Park is turquoise lakes surrounded by snow-capped peaks — it looks photoshopped but it is real. Mount Emei is one of China's four sacred Buddhist mountains, with monasteries scattered through a forest of ancient trees. Or just stay in Chengdu and eat — nobody will judge you.</li>
      <li><strong>Yunnan</strong> (6–10 days): China's most diverse province. Kunming (spring weather year-round), Dali (old town with a lake and snow mountains behind it), Lijiang (UNESCO old town, touristy but beautiful), and Shangri-La (Tibetan culture at 3,300 meters). This is a whole trip on its own.</li>
      <li><strong>Hong Kong</strong> (2-3 days): A world-class city that feels both familiar and completely Chinese. Victoria Peak for the skyline, dim sum at a cha chaan teng (local diner), hiking on Dragon's Back trail, and some of the best cocktail bars in Asia. The high-speed rail from Shenzhen takes 18 minutes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="which-city" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🏙️ Which City Fits You?</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Not sure where to start? Here is a quick matchmaker based on what you care about most as a traveler.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-gray-700 border rounded-xl">
        <thead><tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b"><th class="p-3 font-bold">You are...</th><th class="p-3 font-bold">Choose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="p-3">A history buff who wants to walk through 600-year-old palaces and stand on the Great Wall</td><td class="p-3"><strong>Beijing</strong> — China's imperial heart. The Forbidden City, the Great Wall, and the last remaining hutong neighborhoods where life has not changed in a century.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="p-3">A traveler who wants to see the future — the biggest skyline in Asia, the fastest trains, the sleekest airports</td><td class="p-3"><strong>Shanghai</strong> — China's most foreigner-friendly city. The Bund, world-class dining in the Wukang Road area, and an energy that feels like New York on caffeine.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="p-3">Someone who wants to see something truly ancient — older than Rome, older than the Pyramids in a different way</td><td class="p-3"><strong>Xi'an</strong> — 8,000 Terracotta Warriors standing in formation for 2,200 years, a 14km city wall you can bike, and a Muslim Quarter that has been a Silk Road trading hub for a thousand years.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="p-3">A food lover who measures a city by the quality of its street food and the spice level of its chili oil</td><td class="p-3"><strong>Chengdu</strong> or <strong>Guangzhou</strong> — Chengdu for Sichuan málà (numbing spicy), Guangzhou for Cantonese dim sum and roast goose. Both are UNESCO Cities of Gastronomy and both will ruin you for Chinese food back home.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="p-3">A nature lover who wants to hike, see landscapes that look like paintings, and escape the city noise</td><td class="p-3"><strong>Guilin/Yangshuo</strong> or <strong>Sichuan (Jiuzhaigou)</strong> — Guilin for the karst mountain landscape you have seen in a thousand Chinese paintings. Jiuzhaigou for turquoise lakes and waterfalls that look fake but are real.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3">Short on time — you have 3-4 days and want a great experience without rushing</td><td class="p-3"><strong>One city is better than two.</strong> Pick Beijing if you want history, Shanghai if you want modern energy. Do not try to do both in 3 days — you will spend half your trip on trains.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

